WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:22.155
Meanwhile Javert was frustrated that he had missed his chance to recapture Jean Valjean.

2
00:00:22.156 --> 00:00:28.155
Like everyone else, Javert had believed that Valjean had died falling from the ship.

3
00:00:28.156 --> 00:00:34.789
But when he read about the kidnapping of a girl named Cosette, Javert remembered that name.

4
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:41.726
The newspaper also mentioned that her mother was Fantine. Javert decided to visit the Thenardiers.

5
00:00:41.727 --> 00:00:48.964
Monsieur Thenardier was not cooperative. At first he had been outraged when Valjean removed Cosette.

6
00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:53.114
But then he realized there could be trouble if he informed the police.

7
00:00:53.115 --> 00:01:00.606
They might want to know where he had gotten fifteen hundred francs, or they might question some of his business dealings.

8
00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:06.765
So when Javert came nosing around, Thenardier told him that Cosette had left with her grandfather.

9
00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:15.352
Javert was satisfied until he began hearing tales of "the generous beggar" whose description seemed to match Valjean's.

10
00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:24.169
He borrowed the clothes of another beggar, and soon discovered that his suspicions were correct—"the generous beggar" was indeed Valjean!

11
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.765
Javert chased Valjean, but lost his prey in the cul-de-sac.

12
00:01:28.766 --> 00:01:36.662
For a month after Valjean's escape, Javert watched the neighborhood surrounding the old Gorbeau house, but he finally gave up.

13
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:44.757
Years passed while Valjean was happily settled at the convent with Cosette. New tenants came to live at the Gorbeau house.

14
00:01:44.758 --> 00:01:50.337
They were the Jondrette family and a young man named Marius Pontmercy.

15
00:01:50.338 --> 00:01:59.154
The Jondrettes were extremely poor, and all four of them—mother, father, and two nearly grown daughters—lived in the same room.

16
00:01:59.155 --> 00:02:03.639
There was also a son named Gavroche, but he lived in the streets.

17
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:08.313
His mother adored her daughters but cared nothing about Gavroche.

18
00:02:08.314 --> 00:02:16.805
The Jondrettes had formerly lived in Montfermeil, where they managed an inn and went by the name Thenardier.

19
00:02:16.806 --> 00:02:23.051
As for Marius, he had a connection with the Jondrettes, but he didn't recognize it yet.

20
00:02:23.052 --> 00:02:29.543
And now in order to go forward with our story, we must go back into the past.

21
00:02:29.544 --> 00:02:37.186
Readers may remember that Monsieur Thenardier claimed to have been a soldier in Napoleon's army and to have rescued a general.

22
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:43.437
He even painted that scene on his inn's sign. In truth, he had never been a soldier.

23
00:02:43.438 --> 00:02:48.773
After the Battle of Waterloo, all he did was loot the bodies of the dead!

24
00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:54.694
But one officer—a colonel, not a general—was still alive.

25
00:02:54.695 --> 00:02:59.781
As he went through the man's pockets, Thenardier accidentally revived him.

26
00:02:59.782 --> 00:03:08.257
The officer, whose name was Pontmercy, tried to reward Thenardier with his wallet and watch, but could not find them.

27
00:03:08.258 --> 00:03:13.257
Of course, he did not realize that Thenardier had taken the wallet and watch!

28
00:03:13.258 --> 00:03:20.400
Pontmercy vowed that he would someday find a way to repay Thenardier for saving his life.

29
00:03:20.401 --> 00:03:25.741
After the defeat of Napoleon, the monarchy was restored in France.

30
00:03:25.742 --> 00:03:35.075
Colonel Pontmercy went to live in the town of Vernon. As a supporter of Napoleon, Pontmercy was treated with constant suspicion.

31
00:03:35.076 --> 00:03:42.884
But this was not the worst of his problems. His wife died, leaving him with a young son named Marius.

32
00:03:42.885 --> 00:03:48.892
And the colonel's father-in-law, Monsieur Gillenormand, insisted on taking the boy.

33
00:03:48.893 --> 00:03:56.321
"If you ever attempt to see Marius, I'll disinherit him," threatened Gillenormand.

34
00:03:56.322 --> 00:04:03.947
Since there was a lot of money at stake, Pontmercy did as he was told. But he missed his son terribly.

35
00:04:03.948 --> 00:04:07.706
Gillenormand lived with his unmarried daughter in Paris.

36
00:04:07.707 --> 00:04:14.274
Every few months Pontmercy traveled to Paris and went to the Church of Saint Sulpice.

37
00:04:14.275 --> 00:04:20.020
There he sat behind a pillar where he could secretly observe Marius during the service.

38
00:04:20.021 --> 00:04:29.179
Twice a year Aunt Gillenormand dictated letters to Marius. The boy dutifully wrote them down and sent them to his father.

39
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:36.766
Pontmercy responded with long, tender letters, but Monsieur Gillenormand never showed them to his grandson.

40
00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:45.346
So Marius grew up with little love for his father. To complicate matters, he and his father were on opposite sides politically.

41
00:04:45.347 --> 00:04:51.996
Marius had been raised with the same beliefs as his grandfather, who was a supporter of the monarchy.

42
00:04:51.997 --> 00:04:55.583
His father remained a supporter of the fallen emperor.

43
00:04:55.584 --> 00:05:01.742
By the time Cosette and Valjean were at the convent, Marius had become a young man.

44
00:05:01.743 --> 00:05:13.321
One day his grandfather said, "Marius, tomorrow you will go to Vernon to see your father. He is ill and asking for you."

45
00:05:13.322 --> 00:05:17.447
Marius was so shocked, he didn't question his grandfather.

46
00:05:17.448 --> 00:05:23.315
The next night he arrived at Vernon only to find that his father had just died.

47
00:05:23.316 --> 00:05:29.402
Marius remained in Vernon for two days, pretending to mourn the father he hadn't known.

48
00:05:29.403 --> 00:05:34.815
The sale of Colonel Pontmercy's furniture barely paid for his funeral.

49
00:05:34.816 --> 00:05:41.378
He hadn't left a will, only a scrap of paper, which a servant handed to Marius.

50
00:05:41.379 --> 00:05:47.606
"For my son: Emperor Napoleon made me a baron at the Battle of Waterloo.

51
00:05:47.607 --> 00:05:53.204
I hand that title down to you, knowing that you will bear it proudly."

52
00:05:53.205 --> 00:05:59.450
On the back the colonel had added: "At this same battle, a man saved my life.

53
00:05:59.451 --> 00:06:08.958
His name is Thenardier, and he's an innkeeper at Montfermeil. If you ever meet him, please do whatever you can for him."

54
00:06:08.959 --> 00:06:18.609
Marius took the paper and folded it in his pocket. He went back to Paris, where he was studying law, and thought no more of his father.

55
00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:25.514
The young man had kept up the religious habits of his childhood, often attending church at Saint Sulpice.

56
00:06:25.515 --> 00:06:30.506
One Sunday he was kneeling in prayer when an old man interrupted him.

57
00:06:30.507 --> 00:06:35.847
"Monsieur, you're sitting in my pew," said the old man.

58
00:06:35.848 --> 00:06:41.990
Marius moved away and the old man sat down. After mass the old man approached him.

59
00:06:41.991 --> 00:06:50.237
"You probably think me rude for asking you to move," said the old man, introducing himself as Monsieur Mabeuf.

60
00:06:50.238 --> 00:06:53.323
"I must explain and beg your pardon."

61
00:06:53.324 --> 00:06:58.244
"That's entirely unnecessary, monsieur," replied Marius.

62
00:06:58.245 --> 00:07:05.490
But Mabeuf insisted and went on with his story. "I always feel that mass is better from that seat.

63
00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:11.897
For many years I watched a father sit in that pew and silently observe his son.

64
00:07:11.898 --> 00:07:16.061
The unfortunate man had no other way to see his child.

65
00:07:16.062 --> 00:07:20.569
He always hid behind the pillar, so the boy wouldn't see him.

66
00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:27.950
I could tell that the man worshipped his son, but the boy probably didn't even know he existed."

67
00:07:27.951 --> 00:07:33.545
Marius was puzzled. "Why wouldn't the boy know his father?"

68
00:07:33.546 --> 00:07:41.109
"The father had a rich relative who threatened to disinherit the boy if the father tried to see him.

69
00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:48.037
The father had served under Napoleon; he and his father-in-law were on opposite sides politically.

70
00:07:48.038 --> 00:07:57.696
The father lived in Vernon and his name was something like Pontmarie or Montpercy."

71
00:07:57.697 --> 00:08:01.244
"Pontmercy," said Marius, turning pale.

72
00:08:01.245 --> 00:08:04.521
"Exactly! Did you know him?"

73
00:08:04.522 --> 00:08:07.850
"Monsieur, he was my father."

74
00:08:07.851 --> 00:08:11.347
The old man clasped Marius' hands.

75
00:08:11.348 --> 00:08:21.333
"So you were the child! I'm sorry that you never knew him, but you can say that you had a father who loved you well."

76
00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:30.832
From that moment on, Marius learned all he could about his father. He came to respect the man and to share his political views.

77
00:08:30.833 --> 00:08:40.075
Marius had calling cards printed up with his title, Baron Marius Pontmercy, and wore his father's "will" in a locket.

78
00:08:40.076 --> 00:08:48.663
One day Marius left home in a hurry, leaving behind the cards and locket. His grandfather found them.
